who needs TV when there's a brilliant  meteor shower happening right outside  meteor showers are nature's nocturnal  entertainment and they've been around a  lot longer than your favorite sitcom but  what are they exactly we sometimes refer  to them as shooting stars or falling  stars but that's a far cry from reality  in truth when we see a meteor shower  we're witnessing the fiery demise of  tiny thousand-year-old comet fragments  as they collide with our planet without  comments we wouldn't have most of the  spectacular meteor showers that take  place each year like the stunning  Perseids in August and the breathtaking  Geminids in December  that's because comets are relatively  small and therefore have a weak  gravitational pull so loose particles of  dust and dirt on their surfaces only  need a gentle push and they'll go  tumbling off the comet and into space  that push comes from sunlight as comics  orbit the Sun the pressure from its  light is enough to nudge these particles  loose into the empty vacuum of outer  space once liberated the comet fragments  trace out a tail of dust extending for  millions of miles and they can remain  there for thousands of years  sometimes earth collides with these  Comet dust tails and when that happens  bits of the tiny fragments get swept up  by Earth's gravity and just like  Newton's apple they begin to fall toward  Earth flying through our atmosphere at  blazing speeds of over 100,000 miles an  hour but don't worry most of these  particles are no larger than a grain of  sand and so they burn up into nothing  before they hit the ground  now each meteor shower will usually last  over the course of a couple of weeks but  there's always a peak time to see them  that's when Earth is traveling through  the densest part of the cloud and when  the most dust particles are hurling  themselves through our atmosphere during  some of the best showers we can see over  100 meteors an hour so the next time  there's a major meteor shower turn off  the TV  go outside and look up that's all it  takes to witness cosmic beauty in action  you
 

